{"module":"faith","voice":"male","duration":154.6,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Faith: used to oppress, used to liberate","start":0.0,"end":2.62,"words":[[0.04,0.35],[0.57,0.82],[0.82,0.95],[0.95,1.33],[1.55,1.8],[1.8,1.92],[1.92,2.42]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Religion has been on both sides of this history.","start":3.42,"end":6.5,"words":[[3.47,4.0],[4.0,4.2],[4.2,4.4],[4.4,4.54],[4.54,4.8],[4.8,5.13],[5.13,5.27],[5.27,5.46],[5.46,6.0]]},{"el":"intro","text":"The same Bible that was cut down into a “Slave Bible” carried the Exodus story that enslaved people sang about.","start":6.85,"end":14.58,"words":[[6.89,7.06],[7.06,7.39],[7.39,7.89],[7.89,8.14],[8.14,8.38],[8.38,8.63],[8.63,8.97],[8.97,9.3],[9.3,9.46],[9.46,9.88],[9.88,10.38],[10.38,10.79],[10.79,10.96],[10.96,11.54],[11.54,11.95],[11.95,12.2],[12.2,12.87],[12.87,13.28],[13.28,13.53],[13.53,14.03]]},{"el":"h0","text":"The invisible church","start":14.93,"end":16.63,"words":[[14.98,15.16],[15.16,15.98],[15.98,16.43]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Many enslavers controlled worship, but enslaved people met secretly in “hush harbors” to pray and sing their own way.","start":17.33,"end":25.75,"words":[[17.38,17.72],[17.72,18.49],[18.49,19.26],[19.26,19.77],[20.07,20.32],[20.32,21.01],[21.01,21.43],[21.43,21.69],[21.69,22.37],[22.37,22.54],[22.54,22.8],[22.8,23.22],[23.22,23.4],[23.4,23.74],[23.74,23.99],[23.99,24.25],[24.25,24.5],[24.5,24.76],[24.76,25.1]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In the 1790s Richard Allen and Absalom Jones walked out of Philadelphia's St. George's Methodist Church after being pulled from their knees during prayer for sitting in the “wrong” section; Allen went on to found the African Methodist Episcopal Church in 1816.","start":26.1,"end":42.52,"words":[[26.13,26.28],[26.28,26.43],[26.43,27.53],[27.53,27.97],[27.97,28.26],[28.26,28.48],[28.48,28.99],[28.99,29.43],[29.43,29.72],[29.72,29.94],[29.94,30.09],[30.09,30.9],[30.9,31.26],[31.63,32.14],[32.14,32.73],[32.73,33.09],[33.09,33.39],[33.39,33.75],[33.75,34.05],[34.05,34.34],[34.34,34.56],[34.56,34.78],[34.78,35.29],[35.29,35.58],[35.58,35.73],[35.73,36.1],[36.1,36.24],[36.24,36.39],[36.39,36.61],[36.61,37.12],[37.38,37.67],[37.67,37.96],[37.96,38.11],[38.11,38.26],[38.26,38.62],[38.62,38.77],[38.77,39.28],[39.28,39.87],[39.87,40.53],[40.53,40.89],[40.89,41.04],[41.04,41.99]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Did spirituals carry escape codes?","start":43.42,"end":46.24,"words":[[43.48,43.7],[43.7,44.5],[44.5,44.79],[44.79,45.23],[45.23,45.66]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Spirituals spoke of crossing Jordan and reaching the promised land, and many listeners heard freedom in them.","start":46.94,"end":54.43,"words":[[46.98,47.87],[47.87,48.28],[48.28,48.44],[48.44,48.92],[48.92,49.41],[49.41,49.65],[49.65,50.13],[50.13,50.29],[50.29,50.86],[50.86,51.26],[51.54,51.79],[51.79,52.11],[52.11,52.67],[52.67,52.92],[52.92,53.4],[53.4,53.56],[53.56,53.88]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The popular claim that specific songs like “Follow the Drinking Gourd” were coded maps for the Underground Railroad is debated; historians have found little evidence that song was known before the 1920s.","start":54.78,"end":67.89,"words":[[54.83,54.99],[54.99,55.52],[55.52,55.9],[55.9,56.13],[56.13,56.74],[56.74,57.05],[57.05,57.35],[57.35,57.73],[57.73,57.89],[57.89,58.42],[58.42,58.73],[58.73,58.88],[58.88,59.34],[59.34,59.64],[59.64,59.79],[59.79,59.95],[59.95,60.71],[60.71,61.32],[61.32,61.47],[61.47,62.16],[62.43,63.19],[63.19,63.42],[63.42,63.8],[63.8,64.18],[64.18,64.72],[64.72,64.95],[64.95,65.17],[65.17,65.4],[65.4,65.71],[65.71,66.01],[66.01,66.17],[66.17,67.31]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The broader point, that faith sustained resistance, is well documented.","start":68.24,"end":73.95,"words":[[68.28,68.44],[68.44,68.85],[68.85,69.34],[69.63,69.87],[69.87,70.2],[70.2,70.85],[70.85,71.67],[71.95,72.12],[72.12,72.36],[72.36,73.34]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Faith against slavery","start":74.85,"end":76.94,"words":[[74.89,75.3],[75.3,75.91],[75.91,76.73]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Quakers in Germantown, Pennsylvania, issued one of the first protests against slavery in 1688.","start":77.64,"end":86.13,"words":[[77.69,78.27],[78.27,78.43],[78.43,79.34],[79.63,80.7],[80.98,81.31],[81.31,81.56],[81.56,81.72],[81.72,81.89],[81.89,82.22],[82.22,82.96],[82.96,83.45],[83.45,84.11],[84.11,84.28],[84.28,85.59]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Frederick Douglass drew a sharp line between “the Christianity of this land” and “the Christianity of Christ.”","start":86.48,"end":92.86,"words":[[86.55,87.13],[87.13,87.56],[87.56,87.78],[87.78,87.92],[87.92,88.14],[88.14,88.43],[88.43,88.87],[88.87,89.01],[89.01,89.88],[89.88,90.02],[90.02,90.24],[90.24,90.53],[90.53,90.75],[90.75,90.89],[90.89,91.76],[91.76,91.91],[91.91,92.41]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Nat Turner understood his rebellion as a religious calling.","start":93.21,"end":96.79,"words":[[93.26,93.47],[93.47,93.74],[93.74,94.27],[94.27,94.47],[94.47,95.01],[95.01,95.15],[95.15,95.28],[95.28,95.82],[95.82,96.22]]},{"el":"h3","text":"The movement's churches","start":97.69,"end":99.26,"words":[[97.73,97.89],[97.89,98.51],[98.51,99.05]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Ministers founded the Southern Christian Leadership Conference in 1957, and churches served as meeting halls, schools and sanctuaries, which made them targets: the 1963 bombing of Birmingham's 16th Street Baptist Church killed four girls, and in 2015 a white supremacist murdered nine worshippers at Charleston's Emanuel AME Church.","start":99.96,"end":122.66,"words":[[100.01,100.61],[100.61,101.14],[101.14,101.29],[101.29,101.66],[101.66,102.27],[102.27,102.79],[102.79,103.4],[103.4,103.55],[103.55,104.9],[105.17,105.39],[105.39,105.92],[105.92,106.22],[106.22,106.37],[106.37,106.75],[106.75,107.12],[107.39,107.76],[107.76,107.99],[107.99,108.89],[109.16,109.46],[109.46,109.76],[109.76,109.99],[109.99,110.51],[110.78,110.93],[110.93,112.13],[112.13,112.51],[112.51,112.66],[112.66,113.26],[113.26,113.86],[113.86,114.24],[114.24,114.77],[114.77,115.14],[115.14,115.45],[115.45,115.6],[115.6,115.97],[116.24,116.46],[116.46,116.61],[116.61,117.52],[117.52,117.67],[117.67,117.97],[117.97,118.8],[118.8,119.17],[119.17,119.47],[119.47,120.0],[120.0,120.15],[120.15,120.83],[120.83,121.43],[121.43,121.66],[121.66,122.11]]},{"el":"s3","text":"People of other faiths marched too: Rabbi Abraham Joshua Heschel said that at Selma he felt his legs were praying.","start":123.01,"end":129.7,"words":[[123.06,123.41],[123.41,123.55],[123.55,123.76],[123.76,124.11],[124.11,124.46],[124.46,124.67],[124.92,125.27],[125.27,125.83],[125.83,126.33],[126.33,126.68],[126.68,126.89],[126.89,127.1],[127.1,127.24],[127.24,127.59],[127.59,127.73],[127.73,128.01],[128.01,128.22],[128.22,128.51],[128.51,128.65],[128.65,129.14]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Malcolm X's 1964 pilgrimage to Mecca changed how he saw race.","start":130.05,"end":135.07,"words":[[130.09,130.52],[130.52,130.88],[130.88,131.96],[131.96,132.67],[132.67,132.82],[132.82,133.1],[133.1,133.68],[133.68,133.89],[133.89,134.03],[134.03,134.18],[134.18,134.54]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Today","start":135.97,"end":136.64,"words":[[136.02,136.47]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Congregations run “Souls to the Polls” voting drives, food pantries and reentry programs, and the revived Poor People's Campaign (2018) organizes across faiths.","start":137.34,"end":148.95,"words":[[137.39,138.38],[138.38,138.61],[138.61,138.99],[138.99,139.14],[139.14,139.3],[139.3,139.68],[139.68,140.13],[140.13,140.67],[140.93,141.16],[141.16,141.7],[141.7,141.92],[141.92,142.53],[142.53,143.29],[143.56,143.79],[143.79,143.94],[143.94,144.47],[144.47,144.7],[144.7,145.16],[145.16,145.69],[145.69,146.61],[146.87,147.56],[147.56,147.94],[147.94,148.4]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Faith communities are natural hosts for teach-ins and coalitions.","start":149.3,"end":153.68,"words":[[149.34,149.63],[149.63,150.36],[150.36,150.43],[150.43,151.01],[151.01,151.44],[151.44,151.59],[151.59,152.1],[152.1,152.31],[152.31,153.11]]}]}